#!/usr/bin/python3
# Lucciola System: the one place for what is Lucciola's and not the
# desktop's. (Margine System until 2026-10-18.) Four pages behind a
# sidebar: Overview (is my machine current and healthy?), Startup &
# Security (Secure Boot, the disk unlock, the boot menu, the kernel),
# Login (fingerprint, keyring, automatic login) and Hardware (the CPU
# scheduler). Noctalia keeps the look of the desktop, GNOME Settings the
# standard panels that work under niri; this app never duplicates them, at
# most a row opens the right one.
#
# Everything here already existed as CLI (lucciola-status, ujust
# lucciola-update, -doctor, -kernel, -tpm-unlock, -grub-lock, -autologin,
# -keyring, -secureboot, -scheduler, -report). This app is not a
# reimplementation: it drives the same scripts, so there is exactly one
# definition of each operation and the GUI can never drift from the
# terminal.
#
# Privilege model: the app runs as the user and never asks for a password
# on its own. Privileged actions go through /usr/libexec/lucciola/admin
# under pkexec, which has an authentication agent in a graphical session;
# each verb has its own polkit action (dev.lucciola.admin.policy), so the
# dialog says what is about to happen and an administrator can allow one
# verb without the others. Read-only verbs (*-status) need no password.
# Secrets (a disk passphrase, a boot-menu password, the keyring password)
# travel on the helper's standard input, never as arguments.
#
# Read-only state comes from unprivileged sources wherever one exists:
# `rpm-ostree status --json`, skopeo for the published image, flatpak and
# brew, /sys/kernel/sched_ext, mokutil, the helpers' own status words.
# Decisions that need no toolkit live in
# /usr/share/lucciola/system/lucciola_system.py, which the tests run
# without a display.

